---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Charts & Color | tcplot: Data Visualization in Minneapolis and St. Paul"
    description="Bring a chart whose color you love or hate, then recolor charts together and present the before and after."
>
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
        <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mt-8 mb-12">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">
                Upcoming: Charts & Color
            </h2>

            <div class="mb-6 text-gray-700">
                <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900">
                    Saturday, Jan 16 · 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM CST
                </p>
                <p class="flex items-start gap-2 mt-1">
                    <svg
                        class="w-5 h-5 mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-gray-400"
                        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                        fill="currentColor"
                        aria-hidden="true"
                    >
                        <path
                            fill-rule="evenodd"
                            d="M12 2c-3.866 0-7 3.07-7 6.857 0 4.34 4.686 9.86 6.412 11.74a.8.8 0 0 0 1.176 0C14.314 18.717 19 13.197 19 8.857 19 5.07 15.866 2 12 2Zm0 9.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 0-5 2.5 2.5 0 0 1 0 5Z"
                            clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
                    </svg>
                    <span
                        ><span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                            >The Coffee Shop NE</span
                        ><br />2852 Johnson St NE · Minneapolis, MN</span
                    >
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                Join tcplot for a morning about color in charts, in two acts,
                in the grayest month of the Minnesota year.
            </p>

            <div class="flex justify-center mt-12 mb-8">
                <div class="max-w-sm w-full">
                    <div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
                        <img
                            src="/images/du-bois-1900-taxable-property.jpg"
                            alt="A square divided into four triangles painted crimson, brown, deep blue, and black, labeled North Carolina, Virginia, Georgia, and Total, with dollar amounts"
                            class="w-full h-auto object-contain"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mt-2">
                        W. E. B. Du Bois and his students at Atlanta University,
                        <em>Taxable property of Black Americans in three
                        states</em> (1900), hand-painted in ink and watercolor
                        for the Paris Exposition. Four flat colors carry the
                        whole design.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Act one: bring a chart
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
                Your ticket in is one chart and two minutes on its color: a
                palette you love, a heatmap that finally made something click,
                a transit map you could navigate with your eyes half shut, or a
                rainbow colormap you love to hate. By the end of the round
                we'll have a wall of examples to steal from.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">
                Act two: recolor
            </h3>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                After a short primer on how color works in charts (sequential,
                diverging, and categorical palettes, why rainbows invent edges
                that aren't in the data, and how a chart looks to the one in
                twelve men with color vision deficiency), pick a chart, yours
                or one from our starter set, and recolor it. Then show the
                before and after. Or pull a palette from a January window in
                Minneapolis and make it work as a real chart palette.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                Pick your lane:
            </p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-lg text-gray-600 mb-8 list-disc pl-6">
                <li>
                    <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">No-code:</span>
                    ColorBrewer, Viz Palette, Datawrapper
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Code:</span>
                    d3-scale-chromatic, chroma.js, matplotlib and viridis,
                    ggplot2
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"
                        >Accessibility:</span
                    > colorblindness simulators and contrast checkers
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Analog:</span>
                    paint chips, colored pencils, and paper
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                No design experience needed; the primer starts from zero. Bring
                one chart whose color you have opinions about (on a phone, a
                laptop, or paper) and, if you want to build digitally, a
                laptop.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
                RSVP on <a
                    href="https://www.meetup.com/tcplot/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noopener noreferrer"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Meetup</a
                >.
            </p>

            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
                Hope to see you there!
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
